      "severity": "critical",
      "body": "**Issue:** `escapeHtml` was removed and `body` is passed straight into `dangerouslySetInnerHTML`. Any HTML/JS in the comment string executes in the reader\\u2019s browser (e.g. `<img src=x onerror=\\\"...\\\">`, `<svg onload=...>`).\n\n**Failure mode:** User- or API-supplied comment text is treated as trusted HTML. One malicious comment compromises every viewer of the thread.\n\n**Fix:** Do not assign raw `body` to `__html`. Either (1) render a safe markdown subset to React nodes with HTML disabled, or (2) sanitize with a strict allowlist library before `dangerouslySetInnerHTML`, and cover XSS payloads in tests. Restoring `escapeHtml` alone is correct for plain text but will not deliver bold/links \u2014 use a real formatter/sanitizer for that."
    }
  ],
  "summary": "## Verdict\n\n**Do not merge** \u2014 this change introduces a straightforward stored XSS by removing HTML escaping and feeding the raw `body` string into `dangerouslySetInnerHTML`.\n\n## Analysis\n\n`Comment` previously escaped `&`, `<`, and `>` before assigning to `__html`, which made the `dangerouslySetInnerHTML` usage safe for plain text. The diff deletes `escapeHtml` and renders `body` as HTML with no sanitizer and no markdown pipeline.\n\nAny caller that passes user- or third-party-controlled comment text (the typical case for a `Comment` component) can inject markup such as `<img src=x onerror=...>` or `<script>...` and run script in the session of every reader. The comment about \"bold/links\" does not mitigate that: raw HTML is not a formatting layer.\n\n## What would make this safe\n\n- Parse a constrained markdown subset to React elements (e.g. a markdown library with HTML disabled), or\n- If HTML input is truly required, run it through a well-tested sanitizer allowlist (tags/attrs only) **before** `dangerouslySetInnerHTML`, and add tests for script/event-handler payloads.\n\n**Recommendation:** request-changes",
